<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Dunbar (1296)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1296">1296</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-war-of-scottish-independence">First War of Scottish Independence</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Dunbar was fought on 27 April 1296 near Dunbar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-lothian">East Lothian</a>, where an English army under John de Warenne, Earl of Surrey, routed the Scots after Edward I's invasion and sack of Berwick. Scottish resistance collapsed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> Balliol surrendered and abdicated in July, and Edward took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-of-scone">Stone of Scone</a> to Westminster, opening the Wars of Scottish Independence.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in March 1296 to punish King John Balliol for refusing to support English military action in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. After the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berwick-upon-tweed">Berwick-upon-Tweed</a> he stayed a month in the town to strengthen its defences, and on 5 April received John's renunciation of his homage. The next objective was the Earl of March's castle at Dunbar, 29 miles up the coast. March was with the English, but his wife, Marjory Comyn, sister of the Earl of Buchan, let the Scots occupy the castle. Edward sent John de Warenne, Earl of Surrey, Balliol's own father-in-law, with a strong force of knights to besiege it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The garrison appealed to King John at Haddington, and his army, or a large part of it, marched to its relief without him. The battle seems to have been fought between two bodies of mounted men-at-arms: one of the four formations of the English cavalry against most of the Scottish cavalry, led in part by the Comyns. The Scots held high ground to the west.</p><p>As Surrey's men crossed a gully cut by the Spott Burn their ranks broke up, and the Scots, believing the English were leaving the field, charged downhill in disorder. They found Surrey's force reformed on Spottsmuir and were routed in a single charge. The action was brief and probably not very bloody: the only casualty of note was Sir Patrick Graham, though about 100 Scottish lords, knights and men-at-arms were captured. An English report of over ten thousand Scottish dead probably confuses Dunbar with Berwick.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The next day Edward arrived and the castle surrendered, giving up John Comyn, Earl of Buchan, the earls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atholl">Atholl</a>, Ross and Menteith and 130 knights and esquires, all sent into captivity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The rest of the campaign was a mopping-up. James, the High Steward, gave up Roxburgh without a fight, Edinburgh Castle held out for a week, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stirling">Stirling</a> was found deserted but for a porter. At Montrose on 8 July Antony Bek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Durham, tore the arms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> from Balliol's surcoat, giving him the nickname <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-balliol">Toom Tabard</a>, empty coat. The battlefield was added to the Inventory of Historic Battlefields in Scotland in 2012.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Dunbar (1296)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
